36% of Global VC Is Now Physics-Bound
In March 2026, Celesta Capital announced a ₹2,000 Crore ($240 Million) India-focused deeptech fund. The timing was not aspirational. It was arithmetic.

Deep technology investments now command 36% of total global venture capital, according to Boston Consulting Group: a threefold expansion from the ~10% baseline a decade ago. AI-related deployments alone absorbed 61% of all global VC in 2025. The driver is not software. It is silicon, photonics, and thermodynamics. McKinsey estimates that bringing 124 Gigawatts of AI compute online by 2030 will require $5.2 Trillion in global investment. Sixty percent of that flows directly into physical hardware accelerators, high-bandwidth memory, advanced packaging, liquid cooling. Concurrently, U.S. defense tech venture capital hit $21.4 Billion across 306 deals in 2025, tripling in two years.Global proof earns attention. India proof earns trust.

87 Deals. $2.5 Billion Pledged. One Alliance.
The Indian numbers are not trailing indicators. They are leading. Deeptech deals in India expanded to 87 in 2025, up from 76 the prior year. The sector now constitutes 15% of total Indian PE/VC flows; a nearly fourfold increase from 4% in 2016. The India Deep Tech Alliance, co-founded by Celesta Capital alongside Accel, Premji Invest, and Blume Ventures, has aggregated $2.5 Billion in commitments over five years, with $1 Billion fast-tracked specifically for AI deployment over three years. In 2025 alone, 188 AI funding deals totaling $1.22 Billion closed domestically, a 58% year-over-year jump. NVIDIA, Applied Materials, Lam Research, Micron, and Larsen & Toubro have entered the IDTA as corporate strategic partners. The capital is not speculative. It has counterparties.

